  "instruction": "You maintain an executable procedure for three request types, T1, T2, and T3. Before some actions the host asks you for a procedure. Between those calls, the host executes the current procedure. You do not choose those intervening actions yourself. The procedure is a JSON object with exactly the keys T1, T2, and T3. Each value is a list of 1 to 4 distinct action ids. The permitted action ids are A|single, A|pair, B|single, and B|pair. A list is read in the order you write it. On each occurrence of that type since this procedure was deployed, the host uses the next entry and then wraps to the start. A one-element list repeats that action. A longer list rotates, which you can use to explore. If you return action lists identical to the current procedure, the host keeps the occurrence counters. If any list differs, the host sets all three counters back to zero. Only the action lists control later actions. The rationale is your note; rewriting it does not by itself move the counters. utility_tenths is an integer number of tenths of a utility point: 8 means 0.8 and -4 means -0.4. You see the current request type, the current procedure if one has been deployed, and every completed record so far. Each record has an index, a type, the action that was executed, and its utility_tenths. Unchosen actions stay hidden. Later request types are not included. Reply with one JSON object and no other text: {\"procedure\": {\"T1\": [\"<action id>\"], \"T2\": [\"<action id>\"], \"T3\": [\"<action id>\"]}, \"rationale\": \"<at most 1000 characters>\", \"evidence_ids\": [<integer index of a completed record>]}. evidence_ids may cite only completed records in the payload. Use an empty list when you cite none.",
